{"format":"local-vault-ai-portable-chunks-v1","version":1,"chunks":[{"id":"6 - Full Notes/API Authentication Controller.md::0","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/API Authentication Controller.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"api authentication controller api authentication controller","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/API Authentication Controller.md","fileName":"API Authentication Controller.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"API Authentication Controller","heading":"Document","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"2026-09-27 11:15\n\nStatus: #baby\n\nTags: [[ASP.NET Core API Security and Caching]]","tags":[],"links":[],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/API Authentication Controller.md::1","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/API Authentication Controller.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSearchName":"api authentication controller api authentication controller","filePath":"6 - Full Notes/API Authentication Controller.md","fileName":"API Authentication Controller.md","folder":"6 - Full Notes","title":"API Authentication Controller","heading":"API Authentication Controller","sectionNumber":"","sectionTitle":"","content":"An API authentication controller accepts a credential exchange, verifies the presented identity information, and returns a token or an explicit authentication failure. It creates the initial security credential used on later protected API requests.\n\nThe controller should expose a narrow transport contract and delegate identity verification and token construction to dedicated services. Error responses must avoid revealing whether a particular account exists or which credential detail failed.","tags":[],"links":[],"properties":[],"pageStart":0,"pageEnd":0,"embeddingF32":""},{"id":"6 - Full Notes/API Authentication Controller.md::2","sourceKey":"6 - Full Notes/API Authentication Controller.md","sourceType":"markdown","sectionKey":"__none__","sourceSe